<p>I&rsquo;m not a big fan of using a mouse because it forces me to move my hands from the home row.
I have similar stance on trackpads, although they are a bit better.</p>
<p>What really makes me feel good is trackpoints. They are probably less accurate but way more comfortable for me.
Plus my workflow is mostly keyboard driven and I only need to move the cursor occasionally.</p>
<p>Below is a list of keyboards I used that have trackpoint.</p>
<h2 id="thinkpad-keyboard">Thinkpad keyboard</h2>
<p><a target="_blank" href="https://codelearn.me/assets/img/thinkpad-keyboard.jpg"><img width="600px" src="https://codelearn.me/assets/img/thinkpad-keyboard.jpg"/></a></p>
<p>This was the first external keyboard I bought after switching from my Thinkpad X1 Carbon to Dell XPS 9500.
The discomfort of using dell&rsquo;s trackpad made me buy this keyboard.</p>
<p>I love it. One of the best keyboards I had. The only downside is it&rsquo;s not programmable.<br>
It&rsquo;s very important for me to
make CapsLock button behave as Control on the keyboard level, because when I connect it to another machine I want my keyboard
to function as I used to. It made this keyboard not fully portable for me as that remaping friction caused quite a bit of frustration on my end.</p>
<p>Other than that - perfect.</p>
<h2 id="tex---shura">Tex - Shura</h2>
<p><a target="_blank" href="https://codelearn.me/assets/img/shura.webp"><img width="600px" src="https://codelearn.me/assets/img/shura.webp"/></a></p>
<p>Another nice keyboard I have (mine has one solid space bar though). It is programmable so that issue with the CapsLock mapping
is eliminated with this keyboard.</p>
<p>Unfortunately for me, I can&rsquo;t use it for long period of time. My wrists start to hurt after using it for a few weeks.
I had to switch to my laptop&rsquo;s keyboard (M1 Mac book), though occasionally I use Shura for a couple of days at a time.</p>
<p>It&rsquo;s not the first mechanical keyboard where I discovered I have issues with using them. They always cause quite discomfort for my hands
even though it is very comfortable from &ldquo;home row perspective&rdquo;.</p>
<p>I got some recommendations from my colleagues to use palm rest and it does solve the issue but it creates another point of discomfort
as my portability is reduced.<br>
At work I have meetings and I want to grab my laptop+keyboard which I don&rsquo;t like but still can do.
Grabbing laptop+keyboard+palmrest is too much.</p>
<h2 id="not-sure-what-my-other-options-are">Not sure what my other options are</h2>
<p>I&rsquo;m looking at low profile mechanical keyboards now but there aren&rsquo;t that many options that I can easily try without
paying too much.</p>
<p>There is <a href="https://old.reddit.com/r/ErgoMechKeyboards/comments/oisnad/i_made_this_santoku_gen_2_trackpoint_as_a/">Santoku Gen 2</a> that I might try one day.</p>
<p>Unfortunately the market of keyboards with trackpoints isn&rsquo;t too big.</p>